-- title:
--   Frobenius traces generate T: surjectivity of φ : R → T
-- statement:
--   Fix $N\ge 1$ (a `NeZero` instance) and a set $S$ of natural numbers, let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, and let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from the project's weight-two Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) (the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(S_2(\Gamma_0(N)))$ generated by the project's set `heckeGenerators N 2 S`) to the residue field of $\mathcal O$. Let $T$ be a commutative local noetherian ring, adically complete for its maximal ideal, which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra via a local homomorphism and is finite and free as an $\mathcal O$-module. The hypothesis $H$ is a [`CuspForm.HeckeGaloisRepDatum N S 𝒪 θ T`](def/CuspForm_HeckeGaloisRepDatum.html#L8), i.e. the data of a ring homomorphism $\pi$ from the Hecke algebra to $T$ whose composite with the residue map of $T$ is $\theta$ followed by the induced map of residue fields, such that the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra generated by the image of $\pi$ is all of $T$, such that every ring homomorphism $\chi$ from the Hecke algebra to $\mathcal O$ reducing to $\theta$ factors as $\psi\circ\pi$ for some $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : T\to\mathcal O$, such that $\mathcal O\to T\to T/\mathfrak m_T$ is surjective, together with a $T$-valued adic Galois representation $H.\rho$ (a free $T$-module of rank $2$ with a monoid homomorphism from $\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb Q}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ into its endomorphisms, adically continuous in the project's sense) whose characteristic polynomial at every Frobenius element at a prime $\ell$ with $\ell\nmid N$ and $\ell\notin S$ is $X^2-\pi(T_\ell)X+\ell$, and whose residual representation is absolutely irreducible. Assume further $hS$: every prime $q$ dividing $N$ lies in $S$. Let $R$ be a commutative local $\mathcal O$-algebra, $\rho_R$ an adic Galois representation over $R$, and $\varphi : R\to T$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map whose underlying ring map is local, such that the base change of $\rho_R$ along $\varphi$ is equivalent to $H.\rho$ (equivalence meaning the existence of a $T$-linear isomorphism intertwining the two Galois actions). The conclusion is that $\varphi$ is surjective as a map of sets. Note that no universal property of $R$ is assumed and no completeness or finiteness of $R$ is used.
--
--   This is the surjectivity of the map from a deformation ring onto a Hecke ring in Wiles's method, resting on the fact that the Hecke ring is generated by traces of Frobenius. The formal statement is shaped more generally than the textbook one: $R$ is an arbitrary local $\mathcal O$-algebra carrying any adic Galois representation whose base change to $T$ is equivalent to the one in the datum, and the only properties of $T$ used are the Frobenius characteristic-polynomial relation and the generation of $T$ over $\mathcal O$ by the image of $\pi$; the remaining hypotheses on $\mathcal O$ and $T$ are those needed to form the datum. The hypothesis $hS$ is what confines the generators to the operators $T_\ell$ at primes away from $N$, for which the relation is available. It is applied in [`GaloisRep.DeformationRingData.exists_surjective_algHom_of_heckeGaloisRepDatum`](thm.html#GaloisRep.DeformationRingData.exists_surjective_algHom_of_heckeGaloisRepDatum), which produces a surjection from a deformation ring onto $T$, and thence in the statements attaching Hecke data to semistable Weierstrass curves.
